const stories=[
{r:'europe',t:'The Great Work \u2014 Alchemy & the Search for the Soul',p:'Egypt, Arabia, Europe \u2014 3rd century CE to 17th century',tag:'Alchemy',b:`The popular image of the alchemist as a fraud chasing literal gold misses the profound truth at the heart of the tradition: <strong>the transmutation of lead into gold was always also a metaphor</strong> for the transformation of the base human soul into something divine. The greatest alchemists said so explicitly. Paracelsus wrote that the purpose of alchemy was not to make gold but to make medicine. Michael Maier, court physician to Emperor Rudolf II, wrote that the <em>Philosopher\u2019s Stone</em> was an image of human perfection. The gold was the soul, and the <em>Magnum Opus</em> \u2014 the Great Work \u2014 was the work of becoming.<br><br>The tradition begins in <strong>Hellenistic Egypt</strong> around the 3rd century CE, fusing Greek philosophy, Egyptian metallurgy, and Jewish mysticism. The foundational text, attributed to the legendary <em>Hermes Trismegistus</em>, opens with the most famous phrase in all of occult literature: <strong>\u201CAs above, so below; as within, so without.\u201D</strong> This principle \u2014 that the macrocosm of the universe mirrors the microcosm of the human soul \u2014 is the entire philosophical foundation of alchemy. What happens in the crucible happens in the alchemist. The Great Work cannot be performed on matter alone.<br><br>Islamic scholars preserved and extended the tradition through the medieval period. <strong>J\u0101bir ibn \u1e24ayy\u0101n</strong> (Jabir, or Geber in Latin) \u2014 working in Baghdad around 800 CE \u2014 produced the most systematic body of chemical-alchemical writing of the medieval world, including descriptions of distillation, crystallization, and the preparation of acids that anticipate modern chemistry by a thousand years. His work reached Europe in Latin translation and ignited the Renaissance alchemical tradition.<br><br>The four stages of the Great Work describe both a chemical process and a spiritual journey: <strong>Nigredo</strong> \u2014 the blackening, the death of the old self, the confrontation with darkness; <strong>Albedo</strong> \u2014 the whitening, purification, the washing away of impurity; <strong>Citrinitas</strong> \u2014 the yellowing, the dawn, the first light of wisdom; <strong>Rubedo</strong> \u2014 the reddening, the full manifestation of the perfected self, the gold. Carl Jung spent decades arguing that these stages map precisely onto the psychological process of <em>individuation</em> \u2014 the integration of the unconscious self. He was not wrong to notice the resemblance. The alchemists were mapping the inner life using the language of matter, because in their cosmology, matter and soul were the same substance at different densities.`},
{r:'europe',t:'Merlin',p:'Britain, 5th\u20136th century CE',tag:'Arch-Wizard',b:`<strong>Merlin</strong> first appears in Welsh poetry as <em>Myrddin Wyllt</em> \u2014 a prophet driven mad by battle who fled to the forest and gained prophecy from suffering. Geoffrey of Monmouth's 1136 <em>Historia Regum Britanniae</em> made him the son of a human woman and an <strong>incubus</strong>. He orchestrated Arthur's conception, relocated Stonehenge from Ireland, forged the Round Table. His end: imprisoned in a crystal cave by <strong>Nimue</strong>, the Lady of the Lake, who turned his own magic against him. Some texts say he sleeps still, waiting to return when Britain needs him most \u2014 the archetype of the wounded magician: power rooted in loss, knowledge inseparable from loneliness.`},
{r:'europe',t:'Aleister Crowley',p:'England, 1875\u20131947',tag:'The Great Beast',b:`<strong>Aleister Crowley</strong> called himself the Great Beast 666 and the Wickedest Man in the World. In 1904 in Cairo he received a three-day transmission from a being called <strong>Aiwass</strong> that became <em>The Book of the Law</em>, founding his system <em>Thelema</em>: <strong>"Do what thou wilt shall be the whole of the Law."</strong> He led the Ordo Templi Orientis, produced the most comprehensive system of Western ceremonial magic ever assembled by one person, and appeared on the cover of Sgt. Pepper's. Jimmy Page bought his house on Loch Ness. He died broke, reportedly saying "I am perplexed." His influence on Wicca, modern Satanism, and popular culture is immeasurable and uncomfortable.`},
{r:'europe',t:'Harry Houdini',p:'Hungary/USA, 1874\u20131926',tag:'Escapist & Skeptic',b:`<strong>Harry Houdini</strong> \u2014 born Erik Weisz in Budapest \u2014 escaped handcuffs, locked milk cans, buried coffins, and the Chinese Water Torture Cell. He was also on a lifelong crusade to prove that no medium had genuine supernatural powers. After his mother died in 1913 he attended hundreds of s\u00e9ances. Every single one was fraudulent. He exposed them publicly. He died on Halloween 1926. He left his wife Bess a secret code. For ten years she held annual Halloween s\u00e9ances. In 1936 she stopped: <em>"Houdini did not come through. My last hope is gone."</em>`},
{r:'europe',t:'Eisenheim the Illusionist',p:'Vienna, late 19th century (Millhauser, 1990; film 2006)',tag:'Master Illusionist',b:`<strong>Eisenheim</strong> \u2014 born Eduard Abramowitz \u2014 first encountered magic as a child when a conjurer vanished without trace. That disappearance became his obsession. His later illusions confounded police investigators: spirits the audience could physically touch, whispering names only they could know. His greatest act \u2014 his last \u2014 was walking to the center of his stage and gradually ceasing to exist, feature by feature, until only light remained. Whether based on a real Viennese figure is debated. Vienna in the 1890s was genuinely a city of occult obsession \u2014 Freud mapping the unconscious a few streets away.`},
{r:'europe',t:'Walpurgisnacht',p:'Germany & Scandinavia \u2014 April 30th annually',tag:'Sabbat',b:`On April 30th, witches were believed to fly to the <strong>Brocken</strong> \u2014 highest peak of the Harz Mountains \u2014 for their great sabbat with the Devil. Named for <strong>Saint Walburga</strong>, an English nun canonized on May 1st, 779 CE \u2014 her feast day placed deliberately over the pagan spring fire festival to suppress it. It failed. Goethe immortalized the Brocken sabbat in <em>Faust</em>. Real accusations of attending it sent real people to real executions. Today it is celebrated across Germany and Scandinavia with bonfires, witch costumes, and festivals. The Harz towns sell out of broomsticks weeks before April 30th.`},
{r:'europe',t:'R\u00fcbezahl \u2014 The Turnip Counter',p:'Silesia & Bohemia, recorded from 1561',tag:'Mountain Spirit',b:`<strong>R\u00fcbezahl</strong> \u2014 "Turnip Counter" \u2014 haunts the Krk ono\u0161e Mountains on the German-Czech-Polish border. He controls weather and appears as a giant, gnome, raven, or wandering old man. In the famous legend he kidnapped princess Emma and transformed his <strong>mountain turnips into her friends and family</strong> \u2014 but as the turnips aged and withered, the conjured people decayed with them. Emma escaped and shouted "R\u00fcbezahl!" at him \u2014 the most insulting name possible. You are not supposed to say it on the mountain. His gifts transform based on your character: root vegetables given to an honest person become gold; to a greedy one, they rot. Collected by the Brothers Grimm, 1816.`},
{r:'europe',t:'Rumpelstilzchen',p:'Germany, first recorded 1577; Brothers Grimm, 1812',tag:'Imp & Trickster',b:`<strong>Rumpelstilzchen</strong> \u2014 "little rattle-stilt" \u2014 a noisy limping goblin. The story type is nearly <strong>4,000 years old</strong>. A miller lies his daughter spins straw into gold; an imp does it for her in exchange for her <strong>firstborn child</strong>. She can keep the child if she discovers his name in three days. A messenger overhears him dancing alone singing his own name. The 1857 Grimm ending: <em>"in his rage he drove his right foot into the ground to his waist, then seized his left foot with both hands and tore himself in two."</em> The core magic: knowing a being's true name gives you complete power over it \u2014 one of the oldest magical beliefs in the world, appearing in Egyptian magic, Hebrew Kabbalah, and European fairy tradition.`},
{r:'europe',t:'Circe of Aeaea',p:'Ancient Greece, ~800 BCE',tag:'Sorceress',b:`Daughter of Helios, <strong>Circe</strong> transformed Odysseus's sailors into swine using <em>pharmakeia</em> \u2014 the word that gives us "pharmacy." She later taught Odysseus necromancy, making her history's first recorded teacher of communication with the dead. She is the archetype of the witch who is neither villain nor ally but something altogether more unsettling: a goddess who chooses to live among mortals.`},
{r:'europe',t:'Morgan le Fay',p:'Arthurian legend, ~500\u20131000 CE',tag:'Enchantress',b:`Half-sister to King Arthur and ruler of Avalon, <strong>Morgan le Fay</strong> could shapeshift, fly, and mastered <em>glamour</em> \u2014 the magic of illusion. Early Welsh texts portray her as Arthur's greatest healer. Medieval writers recast her as a villain \u2014 mirroring the Church's systematic demonization of women's magical knowledge. Her name derives from Celtic roots meaning "sea-born.""`},
{r:'europe',t:'John Dee & Edward Kelley',p:'England, 1582\u20131587',tag:'Court Magician',b:`Elizabeth I's court astronomer <strong>John Dee</strong> and scryer <strong>Edward Kelley</strong> conducted hundreds of s\u00e9ances using a black Aztec obsidian mirror and crystal balls (both now in the British Museum). Kelley claimed to receive the <em>Enochian</em> language \u2014 a complete angelic alphabet still actively used in ceremonial magic. Their partnership ended when angels commanded them to share their wives.`},
{r:'europe',t:'Nicolas Flamel',p:'France, 1330\u20131418',tag:'Alchemist',b:`A real Parisian scrivener who became the most legendary alchemist of the Middle Ages. <strong>Flamel</strong> claimed to have decoded a mysterious manuscript \u2014 the <em>Book of Abraham the Jew</em> \u2014 over 21 years, and to have produced the Philosopher's Stone and the Elixir of Life. His tombstone survives in the Mus\u00e9e de Cluny, Paris. He appears in Harry Potter and Fullmetal Alchemist. His real substantial charitable donations later in life remain historically unexplained.`},
{r:'europe',t:'Hildegard von Bingen',p:'Germany, 1098\u20131179',tag:'Visionary',b:`Abbess, composer, naturalist, and mystic. <strong>Hildegard</strong> recorded her visions in <em>Scivias</em> and compiled <em>Physica</em> \u2014 a detailed herbal cataloguing the spiritual and medicinal properties of plants, stones, and animals. Her <em>viriditas</em> \u2014 "greening power" \u2014 treated the life-force of nature as divine. Canonized in 2012. Named a Doctor of the Church \u2014 one of only four women ever. Her visions are now studied as possible migraine auras.`},
{r:'asia',t:'Abe no Seimei',p:'Japan, 921\u20131005 CE',tag:'Onm\u014dji',b:`The greatest <strong>onm\u014dji</strong> (court wizard) of the Heian period, said to be the son of a kitsune fox spirit. He served five emperors as astrologer and exorcist in the tradition of <em>onmy\u014dd\u014d</em>. He allegedly identified demons in human disguise by their lack of reflections. Still venerated; his shrine in Kyoto draws thousands annually.`},
{r:'asia',t:'The Nine-Tailed Fox',p:'China, Korea, Japan \u2014 600 BCE onward',tag:'Spirit',b:`A fox living a thousand years grows nine tails and the power of illusion and shapeshifting. In China the <em>huli jing</em> appears in texts from 600 BCE; in Korea the <em>gumiho</em> is hungry and dangerous; in Japan the <em>kitsune</em> serves the rice deity Inari. One of the most durable supernatural beings in world mythology \u2014 crossing three distinct cultures across 2,500 years.`},
{r:'americas',t:'The Curandera',p:'Mesoamerica, pre-Columbian to present',tag:'Healer',b:`The <strong>curandera</strong> tradition spans thousands of years of Indigenous healing using <em>ruda</em>, <em>copal</em>, <em>romero</em> and ritual to cure <em>susto</em> (soul fright) and <em>mal de ojo</em> (evil eye). Spanish colonizers attempted to eradicate it by equating it with witchcraft. It survived underground. Over 200,000 practitioners are active in Latin America today.`},
{r:'americas',t:'Tituba of Salem',p:'Salem, Massachusetts, 1692',tag:'Accused',b:`An enslaved woman from Barbados, <strong>Tituba</strong> was the first accused in the Salem crisis and the first to confess \u2014 likely under extreme coercion. Her testimony described a tall man in dark clothing demanding signatures in a book, shaping the entire prosecution's framework. Unlike the 19 who were hanged, she survived. She was sold to pay her jail fees. What became of her is unknown.`},
{r:'africa',t:'The Sangoma',p:'Southern Africa, thousands of years',tag:'Diviner',b:`<strong>Sangoma</strong> are healers and diviners in Zulu, Xhosa, Ndebele, and Swati traditions acting as intermediaries between the living and ancestors (<em>amadlozi</em>). Called by the spirits through illness or dreams, trained for years. Over 200,000 practice in South Africa today, often alongside conventional medicine.`},
{r:'europe',t:'Winter Solstice — The Night the Sun Turns',p:'Worldwide — December 21st annually',tag:'Solstice',b:`On December 21st the sun reaches its furthest point south and the night is longer than any other of the year. Then — almost imperceptibly — the days begin to lengthen. Every ancient civilization on earth built monuments, rituals, and entire cosmologies around this moment. Not because they loved astronomy, but because they were terrified. <strong>What if the sun didn't come back?</strong><br><br>At <strong>Newgrange</strong> in Ireland — older than Stonehenge, older than the pyramids — a narrow passage tomb was built so that on the winter solstice dawn and only that dawn, a single shaft of light travels the entire 19-metre passage and illuminates the central chamber for exactly 17 minutes. The Neolithic builders who constructed this around 3200 BCE understood the solstice with a precision we can barely replicate, and they considered this alignment worth a generation of labor. The message of the monument is unmistakable: the light returns. We have always known it.<br><br>The Romans celebrated <strong>Saturnalia</strong> from December 17th through the 23rd — feasting, gift-giving, candle-lighting, and the ritual suspension of social hierarchy: masters served their slaves at table. The Christian Christmas was deliberately placed here to absorb the festival's enormous popular energy. The Yule log, the Christmas tree, holly, ivy, mistletoe — all are pre-Christian solstice magic about things that stay alive when everything else dies, about fire in the longest dark. The Norse celebrated <strong>Yule</strong> with great fires that burned for 12 days — the 12 days of Christmas — because ancient Romans needed 12 days of lengthening daylight before they could be certain the sun was returning and the new year had begun.<br><br>In Persia, <strong>Yalda Night</strong> — the longest night — is still celebrated with family gatherings, red fruits like pomegranate and watermelon (the color of dawn), and the reading of poetry from the 14th-century mystic Hafez. In China, <strong>Dongzhi</strong> calls families home to eat tangyuan — glutinous rice balls symbolizing reunion and wholeness. The cultural expressions are different. The underlying human experience is identical: sitting together in the longest dark, eating something sweet, waiting for the light.`},
{r:'europe',t:'Summer Solstice — The Peak and the Turn',p:'Worldwide — June 21st annually',tag:'Solstice',b:`On June 21st the sun reaches its highest point and the day is longer than any other of the year. And then it begins to retreat. <strong>The summer solstice is the paradox of maximum power and the beginning of loss.</strong> The sun is at its strongest — and it is already leaving. Every tradition that marks this day knows both things at once.<br><br><strong>Stonehenge</strong> was built over several millennia beginning around 3000 BCE, and its entire orientation frames the midsummer sunrise through the Heel Stone. Tens of thousands still gather there on June 21st to watch the sun rise precisely where it always has for five thousand years. The builders were not primitive. They understood precession — the slow wobble of the earth's axis over 26,000 years — and they built for millennia, not decades. Whatever they believed about this day, they believed it absolutely.<br><br>Shakespeare set <em>A Midsummer Night's Dream</em> on this night for a reason understood by every audience in 1600 that requires a footnote today: Midsummer was universally recognized as the night when the veil between worlds thinned, fairies were active, magic was at its height, and ordinary rules suspended. Bottom becomes a donkey. Lovers lose their senses. The fairy king and queen war over a changeling child. Shakespeare wasn't inventing these associations — he was dramatizing folklore his audiences already lived in.<br><br>Across Ireland, Scotland, and Scandinavia, <strong>Midsummer bonfires</strong> lit every hilltop on June 21st. The fires were jumped for luck and fertility. Cattle were driven through the smoke for protection. Herbs gathered at exactly midnight on midsummer were believed to have triple healing power — especially <em>St. John's Wort</em>, which flowers at this time and was used across Europe to ward depression and evil spirits. In Latvia, <strong>Jāņi</strong> is still the most important folk festival of the year: bonfires, flower crowns, songs sung through the shortest night. In Sweden, <strong>Midsommar</strong> is celebrated with maypole dancing and herring, and more Swedes go home for Midsommar than for Christmas. The summer solstice understood the truth Christmas only borrowed: the returning of the light is worth a festival. The apex of the light — the moment before it turns — is worth an all-night vigil.`},

{r:'europe',t:'The Cottingley Fairies — The Hoax That Fooled the World',p:'Bradford, England — 1917',tag:'Famous Hoax',b:`In the summer of 1917, two cousins — <strong>Elsie Wright, 16, and Frances Griffiths, 9</strong> — took photographs in the garden behind their house in Cottingley, Yorkshire that would fool experts, scientists, and the creator of Sherlock Holmes for over sixty years. The photographs showed the girls surrounded by small winged fairy figures, delicate and impossibly detailed. Kodak examined the glass plates and could not prove they were faked. Sir Arthur Conan Doyle, who had been devastated by his son's death in World War I and thrown himself into spiritualism, published the photographs in <em>The Strand Magazine</em> in 1920 as genuine evidence of fairy existence. The world wanted to believe.<br><br>The fairies were paper cutouts — traced from a children's book called <em>Princess Mary's Gift Book</em>, painted with watercolors, pinned with hat pins. The girls had made them in an afternoon. But the photographs were so beautiful, and the desire to believe so strong, that the hoax held. Frances and Elsie maintained the story for decades, not wanting to disappoint people who had found comfort in them. <strong>Frances finally admitted the truth in 1981, when she was in her seventies.</strong> She said of the last photograph: "It was a genuine fairy." She maintained to her death that she had seen real fairies in that garden, whatever the photographs showed.<br><br>The film <em>Fairytale: A True Story</em> (1997) dramatized the affair. The original photographs are held at the National Media Museum in Bradford. The story endures not because of the hoax but because of what it reveals: a world desperate for magic, for evidence that something more than the mechanical and the terrible existed just at the edge of vision, in the garden, at the bottom of the stream.`},
{r:'europe',t:'The Irish Fairy Faith — The Good People',p:'Ireland — ancient to present day',tag:'Living Tradition',b:`In Ireland the fairies were never cute. They were <em>na daoine maithe</em> — <strong>the Good People</strong> — a name given not out of affection but out of careful diplomacy, because naming them correctly was dangerous and insulting them was worse. They were the <strong>Tuatha Dé Danann</strong> — the ancient gods of Ireland who retreated underground when the Milesians (the ancestors of modern Irish people) arrived, and continued their existence in the <em>sídhe</em> — the fairy mounds, the hollow hills.<br><br>The fairy faith was not mythology. It was a living cosmology that shaped daily life. <strong>You did not build on a fairy path</strong> — the lines of invisible road connecting one fairy mound to another — because the Good People would tear your house down. Farmers left corners of their fields unplowed. Lone hawthorn trees in fields were never cut — they were fairy trees, and those who cut them suffered. Even into the 20th century, road engineers in Ireland would reroute roads rather than disturb known fairy sites. A motorway bypass in Clare in 1999 was rerouted at significant expense to avoid a fairy bush that locals said could not be touched.<br><br>The <em>changeling</em> belief — that fairies stole human babies and left sickly fairy children in their place — explained childhood illness and disability in ways that were tragic in their consequences. But it also reveals the depth of the faith: fairies were not decorative. They were a parallel civilization, as real and as dangerous as any human kingdom, who shared the land with humans under terms that required constant respect.`},
{r:'europe',t:'Shakespeare\'s Fairy Kingdom — A Midsummer Night\'s Dream',p:'England — 1595',tag:'Literature & Lore',b:`When Shakespeare wrote <em>A Midsummer Night\'s Dream</em> around 1595–96 he was not inventing a fairy world — he was dramatizing one his audience already inhabited. <strong>Oberon, Titania, Puck</strong> — these were not his creations. Oberon came from French romance. Puck (or Pooka in Irish) was a genuine spirit of mischief widely known in English folklore. Titania's name Shakespeare may have taken from Ovid, but her nature — capricious, ancient, dangerous, beautiful — was the fairy queen his audiences recognized from their own countryside.<br><br>The play is set on Midsummer Night, June 21st, because this was universally understood as the night when fairy activity was at its peak and the boundary between worlds was thinnest. The comedy of confused lovers, the transformation of Bottom into a donkey, the war between the fairy king and queen — all of it maps onto real folk beliefs about what happened in the forest on midsummer night. Shakespeare's audience would have understood, instinctively, that the forest was not safe, that identity could be lost there, that the Good People played with human desire as easily as a child plays with dolls.<br><br>The play gave the English-speaking world its most enduring image of fairies — tiny, winged, capricious, beautiful, powerful — but the older tradition they came from was none of those things. The old fairies were human-sized or larger, terrifying in their glamour, and not to be trifled with.`},
{r:'europe',t:'The Victorian Fairy Craze — Painting the Impossible',p:'Britain — 1840s–1900s',tag:'Art & Culture',b:`Between approximately 1840 and 1900 the British art world went through what can only be called a fairy fever. <strong>Richard Dadd, John Anster Fitzgerald, Richard Doyle, Joseph Paton</strong> — major artists produced large-scale, intricate paintings of fairy worlds that were exhibited at the Royal Academy and hung in respectable homes. These were not children's illustrations. They were technically ambitious, psychologically complex works that depicted fairy worlds as simultaneously beautiful and unsettling — sensual, violent, dreamlike.<br><br>Richard Dadd painted his masterpiece <em>The Fairy Feller\'s Master-Stroke</em> over nine years while confined in Bethlem Royal Hospital (Bedlam) after murdering his father in 1843. The painting — dense with dozens of fairy figures in a world of compressed grass and seeds, all frozen around a fairy woodsman about to swing his axe — is one of the most extraordinary works in British art. Queen Victoria collected fairy paintings. The Pre-Raphaelites painted them. The Victorians were simultaneously the most rational people in history — building empires, industrializing the world, celebrating science — and the most desperate for evidence of another world just underneath this one.<br><br>The fairy craze was partly escapism, partly genuine spiritual seeking, partly the influence of German Romanticism, and partly the growing sense that something irreplaceable was being lost to the machine age. The fairies were everything the Industrial Revolution was not: ancient, natural, free, mysterious, and impossible to own.`},
{r:'asia',t:'The Fae of Japan — Tengu, Kitsune & Forest Spirits',p:'Japan — ancient to present day',tag:'Spirit World',b:`Japan has its own extensive fairy tradition, though the English word barely fits. The <strong>yōkai</strong> — a category encompassing thousands of supernatural beings — include creatures that map closely onto European fairy concepts while being distinctly Japanese. The <strong>kitsune</strong> (fox spirit) can take human form, bestow luck or ruin, and grows more powerful with each of its nine tails, taking centuries to reach full power. The <strong>tanuki</strong> (raccoon dog spirit) is a shapeshifter associated with prosperity and mischief. The <strong>tengu</strong> — bird-headed mountain spirits with wings — were simultaneously dangerous tricksters and teachers of martial arts to legendary heroes.<br><br>The forest in Japanese tradition is not empty. It is populated by <strong>kodama</strong> — tree spirits whose presence is felt as an echo in old-growth forests, and whose disturbance causes misfortune. The animated film <em>Princess Mononoke</em> (1997) is rooted in genuine Shinto animist belief: every river, tree, and mountain has a spirit, and those spirits have interests that do not align with human industry. The <strong>kami</strong> of Shinto — often translated as gods — are also, in many cases, exactly what a European would call a fairy: local, territorial, capricious, powerful, ancient.`},
];

const spells=[
{c:'protection',n:'The SATOR Square',o:'Roman Empire, ~1st century CE',tag:'Ward',b:`The 5\u00d75 palindrome reading identically in every direction: <strong>SATOR/AREPO/TENET/OPERA/ROTAS</strong>. Found at Pompeii, carved into medieval churches from England to Ethiopia. Inscribed on objects to protect against fire and illness. Its meaning remains unresolved after 2,000 years of scholarship.`},
{c:'protection',n:'Evil Eye Amulet (Nazar)',o:'Mediterranean, ~3000 BCE',tag:'Protection',b:`Blue glass eyes reflecting malevolent gazes back to their source. Five thousand years of continuous use across Phoenician, Greek, Roman, Ottoman, and modern Mediterranean culture. Rule: received as a gift, never purchased; buried or returned to water when it cracks.`},
{c:'love',n:'The Philtre of Venus',o:'Ancient Rome & Greece',tag:'Love Magic',b:`Roman love magic documented by Ovid in <em>Ars Amatoria</em>. Common ingredients: <strong>mandrake root</strong>, hippomanes, poppies, added secretly to wine or food. Pliny documented that the wrong mandrake dose caused permanent madness. Love magic prosecutions survive in Roman law codes \u2014 treated as seriously as poisoning.`},
{c:'divination',n:'Runes of the Elder Futhark',o:'Norse, 2nd\u20133rd century CE',tag:'Divination',b:`Each of the 24 Futhark symbols carried power. <strong>Odin</strong> hung on Yggdrasil for nine days to receive them. Rune casting involved carving on wood or bone, scattering them, and reading the pattern. Specific combinations were used to bind enemies. Still actively used in modern Germanic paganism.`},
{c:'binding',n:'Defixiones \u2014 Lead Curse Tablets',o:'Greece & Rome, 500 BCE\u2013500 CE',tag:'Binding',b:`Over 1,600 <strong>lead tablets</strong> inscribed with curses recovered from wells, graves, and temples. A defixio named the target, invoked chthonic gods, was rolled up, pierced with a nail, and thrown into a liminal space. Surviving examples curse business rivals, unfaithful lovers, chariot racers. One Bath tablet: "May he who stole my cloak become liquid as water.""`},
{c:'divination',n:'Scrying \u2014 The Black Mirror',o:'Medieval Europe & Aztec Americas',tag:'Divination',b:`The Aztec god <strong>Tezcatlipoca</strong> bore a smoking obsidian mirror as his portal to other worlds. John Dee used an Aztec obsidian disc to receive the <em>Enochian</em> angelic language. The method: gaze without blinking into a darkened reflective surface until the visual system begins generating imagery from within.`},
{c:'protection',n:"Solomon's Seals",o:'Jewish Kabbalah & Islamic tradition, ~6th century CE',tag:'Talisman',b:`The <em>Key of Solomon</em> documents 44 pentacles \u2014 geometric seals inscribed with divine names \u2014 for protection, commanding spirits, and healing. King Solomon's legendary ability to bind 72 demons is described in the <em>Ars Goetia</em>. These seals appear in amulets across the Islamic world, where Solomon (<em>Sulayman</em>) is a revered prophet.`},
{c:'binding',n:'Poppet Magic',o:'Worldwide, ancient to present',tag:'Sympathetic',b:`Clay figurines in Mesopotamia; wax figures in Egyptian and Greek papyri; cloth dolls in West African <em>gris-gris</em>; the 1324 trial of <strong>Alice Kyteler</strong> in Ireland charged poppets stuffed with hair and nails. The logic is precise and universal: like affects like. The object IS the person, through correspondence.`},
{c:'love',n:'Knot Spells',o:'Scotland, Norse, African traditions',tag:'Binding',b:`Tying knots into rope, hair, or thread while reciting intentions is one of the most universal magical technologies. Norse <em>v\u00f6lur</em> used knotted cords; Scottish witches tied "witch's ladders" with feathers to transfer illness. Appears identically in Ethiopian amulets and West African charms. Roman brides were ceremonially untied by their husbands using the Knot of Hercules.`},
];

