const grimtexts=[
{icon:'\u{0001f4dc}',title:'Papyri Graecae Magicae \u2014 The Greek Magical Papyri',source:'Greco-Roman Egypt',date:'2nd c. BCE \u2013 5th c. CE',link:'https://archive.org/details/Papyri_Graecae_Magicae',about:'Over 130 papyri from Egypt written in Greek, Demotic, and Coptic. Hidden from destruction by religious authorities. Standard English translation by Hans Dieter Betz (University of Chicago Press, 1986). The most important surviving records of ancient everyday magic.',sections:[
{label:'PGM IV \u2014 The Sword of Dardanos (Love Spell, ~4th century CE)',text:'"I conjure you, needle, by the three names \u2014 ANOCH, ABARBACH, SABBAOTH \u2014 let her, whose name I speak, sleep not, but come to me burning with love and desire, dragged by her hair, her heart, her soul, until she stands at my door..."',note:'The formula mixes Greek, Hebrew (Sabaoth), and Egyptian divine names \u2014 typical of the syncretic magical practice of this period.'},
{label:'PGM I. 54 \u2014 Acquiring a Supernatural Assistant',text:'"Take hieratic papyrus and write the prescribed names with myrrh ink. Wash them off into spring water from seven springs and drink the water on an empty stomach for seven days while the moon is in the East. The writing: KAMBRE, CHAMBRE, HARPON CHNOUPHI, BRIONTATENOPHRI..."',note:'The voces magicae \u2014 magical words \u2014 are a mix of divine names from multiple traditions. Their power was believed to lie in precise pronunciation, not meaning.'}
]},
{icon:'\u{0001f4da}',title:'The Munich Manual of Demonic Magic (CLM 849)',source:'Bayerische Staatsbibliothek, Munich',date:'15th century CE',link:'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munich_Manual_of_Demonic_Magic',about:'The most complete surviving necromancer\'s manual from the medieval period, compiled by learned clerics. Richard Kieckhefer\'s 1998 edition Forbidden Rites provides the best accessible English translation. Catalogued as CLM 849 in the Bavarian State Library.',sections:[
{label:'A Spirit Conjuration (after Kieckhefer, 1998)',text:'"I conjure you, spirit N., by the living God, by the true God, by the holy God \u2014 by the names: Adonay, El, Elohim, Eloe, Sabaoth, Elion, Ia, Tetragrammaton, Saday \u2014 that without delay you come to me in a fair and human form, to accomplish all that I desire of you..."',note:'Divine names stack up like legal authorities creating an escalating chain of compulsion \u2014 reflecting the clerical training of the authors.'},
{label:'On Binding a Spirit into a Ring',text:'"Make a ring of silver in the hour of Mercury, and engrave upon it the seal of the spirit you wish to bind, and his name. Then conjure: Enter this ring, spirit N., and remain within it as I command, by the virtue of all the holy names... Seal the ring by breathing on it thrice."',note:'Binding spirits into physical objects appears in Solomonic texts, Arabian Nights stories, and the Greek Magical Papyri \u2014 the Munich Manual synthesizes all these traditions.'}
]},
{icon:'\u2b50',title:'The Key of Solomon (Clavicula Salomonis)',source:'British Museum / Wellcome Collection',date:'Medieval; Mathers English translation 1888 \u2014 public domain',link:'https://sacred-texts.com/grim/kos/index.htm',about:'The most influential grimoire in the Western tradition. MacGregor Mathers\' 1888 translation from British Museum manuscripts (Lansdowne MS 1203, Add. MS 10862) is fully in the public domain. Full text freely at sacred-texts.com.',sections:[
{label:'Solomon to his son Roboam \u2014 Introduction (Mathers, 1888)',text:'"Treasure up, O my son Roboam, the wisdom of my words, seeing that I, Solomon, have received it from the Lord. Know that the beginning of our art and its foundation is to know what God is, and what Man is. For this reason I command thee to hide carefully these my secrets, and to communicate them to no unworthy person."',note:'The king-writing-to-his-son framing gives the text divine royal authority \u2014 a device common to grimoire literature across Arabic, Jewish, and European traditions.'},
{label:'A Conjuration (Book I, Chapter VI)',text:'"I conjure and command you, O spirit, by the holy names \u2014 ADONAY, ZEBAOTH, ADONAY, AMIORAM \u2014 arise, arise, I command you! By the name PRIMEUMATON, which Moses named and the earth opened and swallowed that which had offended the Lord \u2014 by this name I conjure you, spirit, that without delay you appear before me in a fair form."',note:'Public domain \u2014 Mathers translation, 1888. Full text freely available at sacred-texts.com.'}
]},
{icon:'\u{0001f525}',title:'Aradia: Gospel of the Witches',source:'Charles Godfrey Leland, 1899',date:'1899 \u2014 fully public domain',link:'https://sacred-texts.com/pag/aradia/index.htm',about:'Leland claimed to have received this text from an Italian strega named Maddalena \u2014 a gospel of the witch-goddess Diana and her daughter Aradia. Foundational to modern Wicca. Completely free at sacred-texts.com.',sections:[
{label:'The Charge of Aradia \u2014 Diana\'s words to the witches',text:'"When I shall have departed from this world, whenever ye have need of anything, once in the month and when the moon is full, ye shall assemble in some desert place, or in a forest all together join to adore the potent spirit of your queen, my mother, great Diana. She who fain would learn all sorcery yet has not won its deepest secrets, them my mother will teach her, in truth all things as yet unknown."',note:'This passage directly inspired Gerald Gardner\'s Wiccan "Charge of the Goddess" and much of modern pagan liturgy.'},
{label:'A Spell of the Strega',text:'"Take a lemon and stick it full of pins of different colours, then put it into a cup of water \u2014 leave it out at night exposed to the stars \u2014 saying: I take this lemon in the name of the one I love, and I stick these pins in to prick his heart, that he may feel the pain, and think of me until he comes..."',note:'This spell type appears in virtually identical form across Italian, Caribbean, and American Southern folk magic \u2014 suggesting a genuinely ancient common root.'}
]},
{icon:'\u{0001f319}',title:'The Sixth and Seventh Books of Moses',source:'German folk magic tradition',date:'Published 1849 \u2014 public domain',link:'https://sacred-texts.com/grim/index.htm',about:'A German magical text claiming to be the lost books of the Bible that Moses used to perform his miracles. Enormously influential in German-American communities and in African American hoodoo practice. Available at sacred-texts.com.',sections:[
{label:'First Table of the Spirits of the Air',text:'"In the name of the most high and almighty God, who created the heavens and the earth \u2014 I call upon the great spirit MEPHISTOPHIEL, who has command over the winds and the movements of the air, that you appear before me and answer my questions truthfully and without deception, by the power of the great seal AGIOI, OTHEOS, ISCHYROS, ATHANATOS..."',note:'Brought to America by German settlers, this text became a sacred object in Pennsylvania Dutch hexing tradition and was widely sold through African American mail-order catalogues in the early 20th century.'}
]},
