  "In 1692, the afflicted girls of Salem began their testimony. Some historians believe they ate bread made from ergot-infected rye — a fungus with hallucinogenic effects.",
  "The witch's 'mark' — a mole, birthmark or extra nipple — was considered proof of a pact with the devil. Professional witch-finders charged per trial.",
  "Matthew Hopkins, the Witchfinder General, was responsible for the execution of roughly 300 women in England between 1644 and 1647.",
  "In many European witch trials, the accused were 'swum' — bound and thrown in water. If they floated, they were guilty. If they sank, they were innocent. Either way, they usually died.",
