  {icon:'🌙',title:'The Weaver of Moonlight',theme:'transformation',length:'short',body:'Long before clocks kept time, there lived a woman who wove cloth from moonlight. Every night she sat at her loom on a hill at the edge of the village, pulling silver threads from the sky. The cloth she made could not be torn, could not be stained, and glowed faintly in the dark. People came from distant towns to buy a length of it — to line a cradle, to wrap a wound that would not heal, to give as a wedding gift. One night a king\'s messenger arrived and ordered her to weave a robe for the king himself. She agreed. But when she delivered it, the king put it on and found he could no longer lie. Every false word burned his tongue. He had her arrested. She was brought before him in chains. He asked her: "What have you done to me?" She said: "I wove you truth, Your Majesty. The moonlight holds nothing back." He was silent for a long time. Then he removed the robe and handed it back. "Keep it," he said. "It does not fit a king." She walked home free, and the robe hung in her window until it faded with the last of that era\'s moonlight.'},
  {icon:'🦊',title:'The Fox Who Learned Three Truths',theme:'wisdom',length:'short',body:'A fox who thought herself very clever went to the old tortoise of the crossroads and asked: "Tell me three truths and I will owe you a debt." The tortoise said: "First truth — you cannot outrun what lives inside you. Second truth — the thing you want is rarely the thing you need. Third truth — asking the right question is worth more than any answer." The fox laughed. "These are riddles, not truths." The tortoise said nothing. Twenty years later, old now and quieter, the fox returned to the crossroads. "I came to pay my debt," she said. "You already paid it," said the tortoise. "How?" "You came back," said the tortoise. "Only someone who understood had reason to."'},
  {icon:'🕯️',title:'The Candle at the End of the Hall',theme:'mystery',length:'medium',body:'When Mara moved into the old house on Birch Street, she found a single tallow candle at the far end of the upstairs hall, burned three-quarters down. She left it alone. Over the weeks, she noticed the candle never burned lower — but it was always lit when she woke at night, and always cold and unlit by morning. She asked the neighbors. The oldest one, Mrs. Paige, said only: "Don\'t snuff it." Mara didn\'t. In February, when the pipes froze and burst while she was at work, she came home to find the water had spread everywhere in the house — except for a dry circle around the hall table where the candle sat. Mara placed a second candle beside the first and went to sleep. In the morning both were cold, both were unlit. And from the frost patterns on the window, she could see the shapes of two figures standing in a hall.'},
  {icon:'🌊',title:'The Sailor\'s Bargain',theme:'fate',length:'medium',body:'A sailor caught in a storm with no hope of shore called out to the sea itself — not to any god, but to the water. He said: "If you let me live, I will never speak ill of the sea again. Not of storms, not of cold, not of the dark below." The storm passed. He lived. For forty years he kept his word. When other sailors complained about rough crossings, he was silent. When his wife asked why the sea had killed her brother, he said only: "The sea has its reasons." When he was dying, very old, in a room far from the water, he heard it — the sound of waves, unmistakable, impossible, coming from just outside the window of his inland house. He smiled. He had no fear. "We kept our word to each other," he said. And then he was gone, and the sound faded, and the night was quiet.'},
  {icon:'🌲',title:'The Last Tree Who Remembered',theme:'memory',length:'short',body:'At the edge of a city that had forgotten what it was before it was a city, there stood one tree older than all the buildings. The city planners argued about it for years — it was in the way of a road. One night a child touched its bark and felt warmth, which was strange, because it was January. She pressed her ear to it and heard — something. Not sound exactly. More like the feeling of sound. She could not explain it. She told her father, who told no one, but quietly built a small bench beside the tree and started taking his lunch there. Others followed. Within a year, the tree had a garden around it. Within two, the road had been rerouted. The city planners said it was a traffic study that changed things. The child, now older, knew better. "It was remembering us," she told her father. "And we finally listened."'},
  {icon:'✨',title:'The Wish That Took Seven Years',theme:'patience',length:'short',body:'A girl made a wish on a falling star when she was nine and forgot about it by the time she was ten. At sixteen she met a woman who would become her closest friend. At twenty she found work she loved. At twenty-three she returned to the town where she grew up to care for her grandmother, and on the last night of summer, sitting in the garden, she looked up and saw a falling star — and suddenly, without knowing why, she thought: oh. It arrived. She could not have named the wish. She could not have said what star she\'d wished on or what words she\'d used. But she knew, the way you know a smell from childhood — she knew that this was it. This evening. These people. This life. Exactly this.'},
  {icon:'🔮',title:'The Oracle Who Asked Questions',theme:'wisdom',length:'medium',body:'Most oracles gave answers. The Oracle of Ashfield gave questions. Pilgrims came from far away expecting prophecy and instead received a single question written on a folded piece of paper. They were told not to open it until they reached home. Most were furious. Some threw the paper away. But those who kept it and opened it at home found a question so precisely aimed at the center of what they had not said — not what they had asked, but what they had meant — that they sat down and could not stand for some time. A merchant who\'d asked about a failing business opened his paper to find: "What are you afraid would happen if it succeeded?" A widow who\'d asked about her future opened hers to find: "Who told you this story about yourself?" No one ever reported an answer. Only better questions. And somehow that was enough.'},
  {icon:'🦋',title:'The Butterfly Keeper',theme:'transformation',length:'short',body:'In a village known for difficult winters, there lived a woman who kept butterflies through the cold months. Not in cages — she kept them as memories. Each autumn, before the first frost, the butterflies would come to her window. She would touch each one gently, and something passed between them. The butterflies slept through winter. The woman kept their colors in her dreams. In spring, she would open the window and something would lift out of her — not leave, exactly, more like release — and the butterflies would appear again in the meadow, more vivid than before. Her neighbors thought she was strange. Her grandchildren thought she was magic. She thought she was just a good keeper of beautiful things, which is its own kind of magic and rarer than most.'},
];

