const magicDesserts=[
  {icon:'🍮',spell:'The Oracle\'s Syllabub',real:'Syllabub (whipped cream & wine dessert)',origin:'English / Tudor Court',meanings:['Prophecy','Joy','Clarity'],
   lore:`Tudor witches and court astrologers alike served syllabub at the close of feast — a cloud of whipped cream and sack wine, light as breath. It was believed the bubbles trapped in the cream held <strong>whispered answers from spirits</strong>. You counted your bubbles as you ate: odd numbers meant yes, even meant the question needed more time. Elizabeth I's own confectioners made it for royal banquets.`,
   serve:'Whip heavy cream with sweet sherry or Madeira, lemon zest, and a ribbon of honey until soft peaks form. Serve in crystal glasses with a dust of nutmeg.',
   ritual:'Before the first spoonful, breathe gently over the surface three times and speak your wish quietly. What shapes the cream holds after are said to echo what is coming.'},
  {icon:'🍯',spell:'Merlin\'s Honeycomb Tart',real:'Medieval honey and almond tart',origin:'Arthurian / Cornish',meanings:['Wisdom','Enchantment','Memory'],
   lore:`In Arthurian lore, Merlin was said to have survived his long years in the wild on forest honey and the inner bark of trees — real woodland food. Medieval Cornish recipes for honey-almond tarts were called <em>tartes de brymlent</em>, reserved for high feasts and holy days. The hexagon of the honeycomb was considered <strong>a geometric spell</strong> — perfect order drawn from chaos by bees, the messengers between worlds.`,
   serve:'A short-crust tart shell filled with a mixture of almond flour, raw forest honey, egg yolk, and a pinch of saffron. Bake until golden and fragrant.',
   ritual:'Cut the first slice in silence. Place it on the table before passing any others — it belongs to the unseen guests first.'},
  {icon:'🌹',spell:'The Love Knot Marzipan',real:'Marzipan figures (Marchpane)',origin:'Venetian / Renaissance',meanings:['Love','Binding','Beauty'],
   lore:`Renaissance Venice made marchpane — our marzipan — for love spells and betrothals. Molded into hearts, knots, and clasped hands, these almond-sugar confections were given between lovers as <strong>edible promises</strong>. A marchpane heart exchanged meant more than a ring in Venice — it was a binding of desires. Rose water was worked into the paste because the rose belonged to Venus, and what you ate of love, you became.`,
   serve:'Almond paste with powdered sugar, a drop of rosewater, and a brush of edible gold leaf. Shape into knots or hearts by hand. No baking needed.',
   ritual:'Each person shapes their own piece before eating — the shape you make without thinking reveals what your heart is reaching for.'},
  {icon:'🫐',spell:'The Witch\'s Berry Fool',real:'Fruit fool (whipped cream & crushed fruit)',origin:'English / Elizabethan',meanings:['Protection','Pleasure','The Fae'],
   lore:`Elderberries, blackberries, and blueberries were all considered fairy foods in British folklore — their deep purple-black the color of the space between worlds. <em>A fool</em> meant something whipped into nothing, made light of — cunning women made berry fools as offerings to the fae at harvest time. The <strong>crushed berry swirled into cream</strong> was a portal miniature: the dark and the light spiraling together, sweet and tart, neither winning.`,
   serve:'Fold crushed fresh blackberries or elderberries (cooked with honey to a compote) through cold whipped cream until streaked. Do not fully mix — leave the swirl.',
   ritual:'Never use a spoon to fully combine it. The swirl must remain. To mix it completely is said to close the door the dessert opens.'},
  {icon:'🌙',spell:'The Dark Moon Pots de Crème',real:'Chocolate pots de crème',origin:'French / Court Magic',meanings:['Mystery','Desire','The Shadow Self'],
   lore:`Chocolate arrived in Europe through Spanish explorers in the 1500s, immediately surrounded by suspicion and desire — the Catholic Church debated whether it was too pleasurable to drink on fast days. French courtiers called it <em>la boisson des dieux</em> — the drink of gods — and it became the central ingredient in <strong>love potions across 17th-century France</strong>. Dark chocolate, set in small pots with a quiver at the center, was believed to dissolve the defenses of the heart.`,
   serve:'Heavy cream, egg yolks, dark chocolate (70%+), a thread of vanilla, and a pinch of cayenne. Baked in a water bath until just set — trembling at the center.',
   ritual:'Eat in candlelight only. No phones, no noise. The dessert demands full presence — any distraction is said to break the spell it offers.'},
  {icon:'⭐',spell:'The Yule Star Shortbread',real:'Butter shortbread (ancient grain offering)',origin:'Scottish / Celtic',meanings:['Light','Renewal','Ancestral Bond'],
   lore:`Scottish shortbread descends directly from the Yule bannock — a round oat cake ritually broken at the winter solstice to coax the sun's return. The richness of butter was a sacrifice: <strong>the fat of the land given back to the dark</strong>, to buy back the light. Each piece had to be broken by hand, never cut — a blade on the solstice bread was an ill omen. The star shape came later, from the five-pointed seal of the druids, representing the five sacred trees.`,
   serve:'Pure butter shortbread: unsalted butter, caster sugar, plain flour, pressed into a star mold and baked until barely golden. No vanilla, no extras — it is already complete.',
   ritual:'Break, never cut. Give the first piece to the eldest person at the table, and the last piece to the fire or the earth outside.'},
  {icon:'🍐',spell:'The Alchemist\'s Poached Pear',real:'Spiced red-wine poached pear',origin:'Medieval European / Arabic',meanings:['Transformation','Gold-making','Patience'],
   lore:`Medieval alchemists were obsessed with the color red — the <em>rubedo</em>, the reddening stage, was the final phase before lead became gold. A pear poached in red wine and spice turns from pale to deep crimson before your eyes, and <strong>alchemical texts used cooking as metaphor</strong> — and vice versa. Arabic physicians prized pears for fortifying the liver and the will. The cloves were a symbol of the philosopher's fire: patient, precise, transforming.`,
   serve:'Whole pears simmered in red wine with cinnamon stick, cloves, orange peel, star anise, and a pour of honey until jewel-red and yielding. Serve with a thread of dark honey and a dollop of mascarpone.',
   ritual:'Watch the pear change color as it cooks. The moment it turns fully red, it is ready — take it from the heat then. Alchemical patience: not before, not after.'},
  {icon:'🕯️',spell:'The Samhain Soul Cake',real:'Soul cake (spiced currant cake)',origin:'English / Pagan / Medieval',meanings:['Remembrance','The Ancestors','Crossing'],
   lore:`Soul cakes are one of the oldest Halloween traditions in England — small spiced cakes left on doorsteps for wandering souls on the night of Samhain, and given to "soulers" who went door to door singing prayers for the dead. <strong>Each cake was a soul released from purgatory</strong>. Made with saffron, allspice, and currants, they were imprinted with a cross — not always Christian, sometimes the older solar cross marking the four seasons. The recipe predates the Church borrowing of the holiday.`,
   serve:'Small round spiced cakes of flour, butter, egg, sugar, saffron, allspice, nutmeg, and currants. A cross pressed into each before baking. Served with spiced cider.',
   ritual:'If you wish to honor someone who has passed, leave one soul cake outside on the night of October 31st, with their name spoken aloud three times before placing it.'}
];
