  });

  // Closing
  h+='<div style="background:rgba(30,20,50,.7);border-radius:10px;padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin-top:.8rem;border-left:3px solid #e8d0ff">';
  h+='<h3 style="font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#e8d0ff;font-size:1rem;margin:0 0 .6rem">What These Places Share</h3>';
  h+='<p style="color:#b8a8d8;font-size:.84rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 .7rem">From the Andes to Anatolia, from Irish hills to the Sahara edge, the same impulse appears: <strong>orient the building to the sky</strong>. Align the entrance to the solstice sunrise. Make the calendar stone. Point the passage at the stars. These were not decorative choices. In every case, the builders were encoding their most important knowledge — what time it is in the universe, where the sacred intersects the physical — in the one medium they trusted to outlast them: <em>stone</em>.</p>';
  h+='<p style="color:#b8a8d8;font-size:.84rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 .7rem">They were right. The stones are still here. We read them now with satellite surveys and spectroscopy and ground-penetrating radar, and what we find is that people who had none of those tools observed the sky with such sustained, transgenerational attention that they could encode the position of a star 5,000 years in the future with less error than we can maintain in a modern wristwatch over a decade.</p>';
  h+='<p style="color:#b8a8d8;font-size:.84rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0">The word <em>sacred</em> comes from the Latin <em>sacer</em> — set apart, made holy, removed from ordinary use. A sacred place is one where the full weight of human attention has been focused, generation after generation, across centuries of lives that were shorter and harder than ours. What they were looking for, at Delphi and Kailash and Göbekli Tepe and the Nazca plateau, was the same thing: the place where the visible world became transparent enough to see what lay behind it. Some of them found it. The evidence is in the stone.</p>';
