  h+='<div style="background:rgba(30,20,50,.7);border-radius:10px;padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin-top:.8rem;border-left:3px solid #c0a0ff">';
  h+='<h3 style="font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#c0a0ff;font-size:1rem;margin:0 0 .6rem">What Makes a City Magical</h3>';
  h+='<p style="color:#b8a8d8;font-size:.84rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 .7rem">A city becomes magical when it becomes a <em>container for human aspiration</em> — when the density of lives lived in one place over long time produces something that no single life could produce. Constantinople held the knowledge of Rome and Greece across a millennium of darkness. Baghdad multiplied that knowledge and sent it forward into a future it would not live to see. Timbuktu held manuscripts in the desert for 500 years, waiting for a world ready to read them again.</p>';
  h+='<p style="color:#b8a8d8;font-size:.84rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0">The imaginary cities — El Dorado, Atlantis, Shambhala — are magical in a different and deeper way: they are the cities <em>we have not built yet</em>. Every age projects its longing onto a distant horizon and calls it a city of gold. What we are really describing, when we describe paradise, is not a place to find — it is a standard to build toward. The cities that actually glow in history did so because enough people, for long enough, decided that <strong>this place will be extraordinary</strong>. They were not wrong to decide it. Neither are we.</p>';
