    'Dragon Adventure':'Once upon a time, in the tall mountains where the clouds were always purple, there lived a small dragon named Pip who could not breathe fire. All the other dragons roared and blazed, but when Pip tried, only glittery pink bubbles came floating out. The other dragons laughed so hard the mountain shook.\n\nPip tried everything. He ate spicy peppers. He stood on his head. He practiced roaring in the middle of the night until the stars complained. Nothing worked. Just bubbles — beautiful, shimmery, perfectly round pink bubbles.\n\nOne day a royal messenger arrived: the princess had lost her favorite golden locket somewhere in the deep dark cave on the other side of the mountain. Every dragon who went in to search came back out coughing from the thick black smoke inside. But Pip, who had no fire to make more smoke, walked straight in.\n\nInside the cave, Pip blew a great stream of glittery pink bubbles. Each one floated through the darkness like a little lantern, lighting up every corner, every crack, every hidden ledge. And there on a narrow rocky shelf sparkled the golden locket, safe and sound.\n\nPip carried it back on a cushion of floating pink bubbles. The princess laughed with delight. From that day on the whole kingdom agreed: Pip was the most magical, most useful, most wonderful dragon who had ever lived. And no one ever laughed at the bubbles again. The End.',
    'Mermaid Tale':'Once upon a time, at the bottom of the warmest, bluest part of the ocean, there lived a young mermaid named Pearl who collected things that sank from the world above. She had buttons, a toy boat, a red mitten, and her most prized treasure — a golden shell she had found wedged between two coral rocks.\n\nEvery morning Pearl put the shell to her ear to hear the sound of the sea inside a sea. But one morning something was different. Instead of the usual soft roaring, a real song rang out — clear and sweet and full of light. Every fish who heard it began to dance. Starfish spun on their tips. Jellyfish waltzed in slow circles. Even the grumpy old octopus tapped four of his eight arms.\n\nPearl swam all through the reef and along the sandy floor, carrying the singing shell so every creature could hear it. The song spread like a warm current, moving from fish to fish, reaching all the way to the sunlit surface. Dolphins leaped. Whales hummed along from very far away.\n\nUp above, a little girl sitting alone in a boat heard something beautiful rising from the sea. She leaned over the side and smiled at the glittering water. Far below, Pearl looked up through the light and smiled back.\n\nFrom that day on, every summer evening, if you sat very still on the water, the ocean hummed a little song. And every child who heard it felt, for just a moment, that the sea was singing only for them. The End.',
    'Wizard Quest':'Once upon a time, at a school where the staircases moved on their own and the portraits on the walls gave unsolicited homework advice, there was a young wizard named Finley who was very good at almost everything. Almost.\n\nFinley had one problem: he could never quite keep his spells pointed in the right direction. One Tuesday he was practicing the Transformation of Small Objects and meant to turn his quill into a candle. Instead, with a sound like a very surprised cork, his hat leapt off his head, sprouted long floppy ears, and hopped off the desk. A real, living, slightly confused bunny went bounding down the hall.\n\nThe whole school gave chase. The bunny leaped over the cauldrons in the potion room, bounced through the library, scattered a tower of spell books, and finally came to rest — quite comfortably — right on top of the headmistress hat during the most important faculty meeting of the year.\n\nThe headmistress looked up very slowly. The room held its breath. Then she laughed so hard her spectacles slid right off her nose, and everyone else laughed too, and the bunny looked extremely pleased with itself.\n\nFinley stepped forward, ears red, and apologized. The headmistress said it was the best thing to happen at the school in forty years. She declared the bunny the official mascot and let Finley name him. He named him Hat. Hat lived happily at the school for a very, very long time — and Finley eventually learned to aim his spells properly. Mostly. The End.',
    'Animal Friends':'Once upon a time, on the edge of a forest where the trees had very good manners and always let the wind through politely, three friends decided to throw a birthday party for someone who had never had one. That someone was a very old tortoise named Grumble, who said birthdays were nonsense — but secretly wanted one more than he had ever wanted anything.\n\nA lion named Bramble brought balloons, though he kept popping them with his claws by accident and then looking very innocent. A rabbit named Clover was in charge of the cake, but kept eating the ingredients and having to start over from the beginning, which happened four times. A frog named Splotch set up the decorations and had hung everything upside down because, he explained patiently, things looked very festive from a frog perspective.\n\nWhen Grumble arrived and saw the half-decorated clearing, the lopsided cake with one crooked candle, the three friends covered in flour and balloon scraps — he stopped completely still. His very old eyes went very wide.\n\nHis wrinkled face did something it had not done in forty years. He smiled. A real smile, wide and warm and surprised right out of him. He said it was the most perfect party he had ever seen, because it had been made with love and almost no skill whatsoever, which was the best kind.\n\nThey ate the lopsided cake under the stars. It tasted like the best thing any of them had ever eaten. And Grumble, who had said birthdays were nonsense, had one every single year after that. The End.',
    'Space Magic':'Once upon a time, a young astronaut named Zara put on her silver helmet, climbed into her little ship shaped like a shooting star, and blasted off into the night sky — because she had seen a light blinking from somewhere far past the moon and she could not rest until she found out what it was.\n\nShe flew past rings of ice that chimed like bells when the solar wind blew through them. She flew past a storm the size of a whole country, swirling in slow beautiful loops. A comet waved its bright tail at her as she passed and she waved back, which is the polite thing to do.\n\nFinally she arrived at a small round planet the color of strawberry jam, where everything was made of some kind of wonderful sweet cloud. The mountains were cotton candy. The rivers ran warm and slightly fizzy. The sky was the color of a peach at exactly the right moment of ripeness.\n\nWaiting on the tallest candy mountain was a creature about the size of a cat, with enormous purple eyes and a smile even bigger than that. It was holding a plate of star-shaped cookies. It had been blinking that light for a very long time, hoping someone would come.\n\nZara shared her peanut butter sandwich. The creature shared every cookie. They decided to call the planet Tuesday, because neither of them knew the real name and Tuesday sounded like a friendly sort of word. Zara visited every full moon after that. The creature always had cookies ready. The End.'
  };

  var story=storyMap[type]||'Once upon a time, in a world full of magic and wonder, something extraordinary happened. It was so remarkable that everyone who heard about it smiled for the rest of the day. And the magic never stopped — because magic never really does. The End.';

