    {name:'Raven',emoji:'🐦‍⬛',element:'Air',tradition:'Norse, Celtic, Pacific Northwest, Greek',
     headline:'The Memory of the World',
     lore:'Odin kept two ravens — Huginn (Thought) and Muninn (Memory) — who flew across all the worlds each day and returned to whisper everything they had seen into his ear. This is the oldest recorded description of an intelligence network. Ravens were sacred at the Tower of London because a raven in residence meant the kingdom would stand. In Pacific Northwest traditions the Raven created the world by stealing the sun and bringing it to the people. In Celtic myth the Morrigan, goddess of fate and battle, appeared as a raven. The raven is always associated with the deepest knowledge — the kind that can only be earned, not taught.',
     omen:'Three ravens together means something is about to be decided that cannot be undone. A raven landing on your roof means the house is being blessed and protected. A raven bringing you a bright object (they do this) means you will find unexpected treasure.',
     saying:'"The raven remembers everything. That is why it never seems surprised."'},
    {name:'Wolf',emoji:'🐺',element:'Earth',tradition:'Norse, Native American, Roman, Celtic',
     headline:'The Guardian of the Pack',
     lore:'Romulus and Remus were nursed by a she-wolf — and from that she-wolf Rome was built. In Norse myth, two great wolves chase the sun and moon across the sky. The wolf Fenrir will swallow Odin at the end of the world, and yet — this is important — Odin kept two tame wolves as his companions. The thing feared most is also the thing most intimately known. In nearly every Native American tradition, the wolf is the ultimate teacher of loyalty, family structure, and endurance. Wolf medicine means you know who your pack is — and you would do anything for them.',
     omen:'Hearing a wolf howl at dawn is a call to action — something you have been putting off must be done. A wolf appearing in a dream means you have a guardian watching over your home and family. Two wolves seen together means a partnership you are considering will prove true.',
     saying:'"The wolf does not perform loyalty. The wolf simply never leaves."'},
    {name:'Bear',emoji:'🐻',element:'Earth',tradition:'Norse, Native American, Siberian, Celtic',
     headline:'Strength That Goes to Sleep and Returns',
     lore:'The bear is the only large predator that disappears and returns — it dies every winter and is reborn every spring. This is why the bear is the animal of resurrection across nearly every northern culture. In Norse tradition the berserkers wore bear skins and called on the bear spirit before battle. In Siberian shamanic tradition the bear is the father of the shaman, the first spirit to teach humans to move between worlds. In Celtic myth the word for bear — arth — is the root of Arthur. The greatest king who ever lived was named for the bear who comes back.',
     omen:'A bear appearing in a dream during winter means you need to rest, genuinely rest — stop. A bear appearing in spring means an enormous new strength is available to you right now. Never disturb a sleeping bear; never disturb someone who needs to rest.',
