    {name:'Snake',emoji:'🐍',element:'Water',tradition:'Greek, Egyptian, Celtic, Hindu, Mesoamerican',
     headline:'The Keeper of Secrets Beneath the Earth',
     lore:'The snake sheds its skin and emerges renewed. This is why it was the symbol of medicine (Asclepius carried a staff with a snake), of immortality, of hidden wisdom in every ancient culture simultaneously. Serpents guarded the temple oracles at Delphi. The Egyptian uraeus cobra on the pharaoh\'s crown meant divine protection. In Hindu tradition Shesha, the cosmic serpent, holds the entire universe. The Aztec Quetzalcoatl was a feathered serpent — the union of earth and sky. Whatever cultures thought about snakes, they agreed: the snake knows something the rest of us do not.',
     omen:'A snake crossing your path going left to right means you are about to shed something that has been weighing you down. Finding a snakeskin intact is one of the luckiest omens in country folklore — keep it in the house for protection and renewal.',
     saying:'"The snake has already lived through what you are about to face. It left its old self behind and moved on."'},
    {name:'Hare',emoji:'🐇',element:'Water',tradition:'Celtic, East Asian, Native American, Norse',
     headline:'The Moon Creature',
     lore:'Look at a full moon. In nearly every culture that looked up — Celtic, Chinese, Japanese, Aztec, Algonquin — they saw a hare in the patterns of the moon\'s face. Not a man. A hare. The hare is the animal of the moon, of mystery, of things that happen at the boundary of day and night. In Celtic tradition the hare is so sacred it cannot be eaten. Boudicca released a hare before battle to divine which way to advance. In East Asian tradition, the Jade Hare lives on the moon and pounds the elixir of immortality with a mortar and pestle. The hare appears and disappears — here, then gone — and this is exactly what it means.',
     omen:'Seeing a hare at dusk is a powerful omen of magic entering your life. A white hare is an ancestor visiting. Three hares seen together — called the "Three Hares" symbol, found in medieval churches across Europe — is a blessing of completion and cycles fulfilled.',
     saying:'"The hare lives at the edge of the field, where the known world meets the wild. It has always known the way across."'},
    {name:'Cat',emoji:'🐱',element:'Fire',tradition:'Egyptian, Celtic, Norse, Japanese',
     headline:'The Guardian of the Threshold',
     lore:'Bastet, the Egyptian cat goddess, protected the home, children, and women. Killing a cat in ancient Egypt — even accidentally — was punishable by death. The cat was the first and most sacred of all household guardians. In Norse tradition, Freya\'s chariot was pulled by two great cats. Japanese maneki-neko beckons fortune into homes and businesses. In Celtic tradition, the great Cat Sith — a fairy cat — stalked the Highlands, and if you treated it kindly it left treasure; if you disrespected it, the consequences followed you for years. Cats see things humans cannot. They are watching something in the corners of rooms that we simply cannot perceive.',
     omen:'A black cat crossing your path left to right in the British tradition is lucky — American folk tradition reversed this, but the British version is older. A cat staring fixedly at an empty corner means something is present that you cannot see but that wishes you well. A cat sleeping in a new home means the house accepts you.',
     saying:'"The cat guards the boundary between this world and the next. It takes this responsibility extremely seriously."'},
    {name:'Deer',emoji:'🦌',element:'Earth',tradition:'Celtic, Native American, Japanese, Hindu',
     headline:'The Gentle Messenger',
     lore:'In every culture the deer enters, it carries the same energy: grace, gentleness, an invitation to approach something sacred. In Celtic tradition the deer was the guide into the Otherworld — you follow the white stag and find yourself somewhere that should not exist. In Japanese tradition the deer at Nara are considered divine messengers of the gods, protected for over a thousand years. In Hindu tradition the deer represents the quality of being so alive and present that you cannot be caught or cornered. The Buddha is often depicted with deer — their first appearance after his enlightenment, drawn to that quality of absolute peace.',
     omen:'A deer appearing at the edge of a wood and watching you — not fleeing — is a message of gentleness: approach what you are afraid to approach with softness, not force. A white deer or albino deer is the most sacred omen in woodland traditions across the world. If you see one, make a wish.',
     saying:'"The deer does not fight. It simply steps aside, and the thing that was chasing it runs off a cliff."'},
    {name:'Horse',emoji:'🐎',element:'Fire',tradition:'Celtic, Norse, Greek, Mongolian',
     headline:'The Spirit That Carries You',
     lore:'Epona, the Celtic horse goddess, was so beloved that even Rome — after conquering Gaul — adopted her. Sleipnir, Odin\'s eight-legged horse, could travel between all the worlds — it was the fastest thing in existence. Pegasus could fly to the realm of the gods. The horse in every tradition represents freedom, power, and the ability to go somewhere beyond where you are. In Mongolian shamanism the horse is the shaman\'s spirit vehicle, the drum is called the horse, and the shaman rides it between worlds during trance. White horses are nearly universally sacred. The Uffington White Horse, carved into the English hills 3,000 years ago, is still there.',
     omen:'A horse that approaches you and breathes on your face is giving you a blessing in almost every equestrian magical tradition. Dreaming of a running horse means freedom is arriving. A horse standing perfectly still and looking at you means stop what you are doing and listen.',
     saying:'"The horse will carry you wherever you need to go, but it cannot be forced. It must choose."'},
    {name:'Frog',emoji:'🐸',element:'Water',tradition:'Egyptian, Celtic, Mesoamerican, Japanese',
     headline:'The Bringer of Rain and Transformation',
     lore:'Heqet, the Egyptian frog goddess, was the goddess of fertility and birth — midwives wore amulets of her image. In Celtic tradition the frog is the "Lord of the Earth" and holds the healing power of water. In Mesoamerican traditions frogs are invoked to bring rain. In Japan the frog (kaeru) is a symbol of good luck and safe return — travelers carry frog charms because kaeru also means "to return." The frog undergoes the most dramatic transformation in nature — from fish-like tadpole to air-breathing creature — and this is its oldest magic: the proof that total transformation is possible.',
     omen:'Frogs singing near your home mean rain is coming and with it, abundance. A frog entering your house is considered lucky in most world traditions — do not harm it; guide it out gently. Dreaming of frogs means you are in the middle of a transformation that has not completed yet. Be patient.',
     saying:'"The frog was once something else entirely. It remembers. That is why it sings at the edge of the water."'},
