  html += '<p style="color:#e0a0a8;font-size:.88rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 .7rem"><em>Onmyōdō</em> — the Way of Yin and Yang — was the dominant esoteric system of medieval Japan, blending Chinese Taoist cosmology, Vedic astrology, Buddhist mysticism, and native Shinto spirit traditions into something entirely unique. For over a thousand years, from approximately 700 CE to the Meiji period, the Onmyōji — master practitioners of this system — were among the most powerful figures in Japan. They advised emperors, performed divination, exorcised spirits, chose auspicious dates for battles and marriages, and managed the spiritual welfare of the imperial court.</p>';
  html += '<p style="color:#e0a0a8;font-size:.88rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 .7rem">The greatest Onmyōji in history was <strong style="color:#c03c50">Abe no Seimei</strong> (921–1005 CE), who became a legendary figure so remarkable that the Japanese still tell stories of his supernatural abilities today. He is said to have been born of a human father and a fox spirit mother, giving him the ability to see demons invisible to ordinary humans. His family crest — a five-pointed star called the <em>Seiman</em> — became the most powerful protective symbol in Japanese magical tradition, and is still used today.</p>';
